WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:24.020
- Hello, welcome to Khan Academy Ed Talks.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.920
I am Caroline Hu Flexer,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.530
the CEO and Co-Founder
of Khan Academy Kids,

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.440
which is a mobile app for
children two through eight

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.990
that's focused on literacy, math,

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.980
and social-emotional learning.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.550
Today we are celebrating
National Reading Month,

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.060
and I'm excited to talk
with our special guest,

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.660
Kristi Yamaguchi, who is
a Olympic gold medalist,

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.020
author, and philanthropist.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.640
If you have questions for Kristi,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.420
please drop them into the chat.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.560
And before we get started,
I wanna remind you

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.000
that Khan Academy is a
nonprofit organization.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.560
We're able to do the work that we do

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.380
thanks to the generosity and donations

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.400
from folks like all of you.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.210
So if you're able to give,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:14.530
please go to KhanAcademy.org/donate

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.080
and you'll find a place where
you can make that donation.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.735
And we are so grateful for
anything that you can give

00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:21.590
to our mission.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.380
I also wanted to recognize
the organizations

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.326
that have helped support our
work during the pandemic,

00:02:27.326 --> 00:02:31.713
including AT&amp;T, General
Motors, and Fastly.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.740
Finally, if you want to
listen to this podcast again

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.360
or find previous broadcasts,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.410
you can find them at Homeroom
with Sal, The Podcast

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.063
anywhere you get your podcasts.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.160
So let's get started.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.130
Today I'm thrilled to introduce you all

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.490
to Kristi Yamaguchi.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.345
She is a wife, mother,
Olympic gold medalist,

00:02:53.345 --> 00:02:55.120
children's book author,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.780
and founder of the nonprofit Always Dream.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.760
Kristi Yamaguchi's Always
Dream ensures that children

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.620
from low income families have access

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.430
to high-quality books.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.929
Welcome, Kristi, it's
great to have you here.

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:10.240
- Thank you, Caroline,
it's great to join you.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.290
Thanks for having me.

00:03:12.832 --> 00:03:16.260
- Yeah, so the Winter
Olympics just finished.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.270
This year is your 30th anniversary

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.530
since capturing the gold medal.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.390
What is your most
cherished memory you have

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.830
regarding your Olympic experience?

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.960
We'd love to hear a bit more

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.600
about what the Olympics
were like back then.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.140
- Yeah, so '92 was a while back, right?

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.270
But it was a incredible experience.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.310
I think when you talk to any Olympian,

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.270
one of the most vivid
and fondest memories,

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.260
always marching in the Opening Ceremonies.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.080
And I think just being a part

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.610
of an incredible group of
athletes, not just figure skaters,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:58.384
but the skiers, the speed
skaters, the bobsledders,

00:03:58.384 --> 00:04:02.110
biathlons, it's just an
incredible pool of talent

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.800
and it's so humbling.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.240
And I think there's no prouder feeling

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.940
than putting on our Team USA uniforms

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:14.180
and marching in together
representing our country.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.220
So that, I think, will always be

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.760
an incredible, special moment.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.970
And you know, the Games
themselves are just

00:04:23.281 --> 00:04:26.160
something that's a lifelong pursuit.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.930
And it's so amazing just to be there

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.690
and an honor to represent.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.694
But the competition gets a little intense,

00:04:34.694 --> 00:04:36.720
as you can imagine.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.840
But lots of fun, too.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.460
I mean, I knew I was prepared

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.040
and I was ready for that moment.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.960
And even though my nerves were creeping in

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.870
and giving me a little
bit of that scary feeling,

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.690
I kept telling myself, go out there,

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.990
do what you're trained to do,

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.933
and the preparation's going
to hopefully be something

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.544
I could rely on.

00:05:01.544 --> 00:05:02.820
And it came through for me.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.140
So I feel very fortunate every day.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.260
- Yeah, it clearly did.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.330
And I'm sure there were many life lessons

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.520
that you learned as a figure skater.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.770
What were some of those that carried over

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.643
into your work now in
raising and teaching kids?

00:05:23.652 --> 00:05:25.820
- I mean, that's such a great point.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:29.800
And I think it's all about
having a dream, right,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.530
and really going after those dreams,

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:37.160
and really encouraging kids
to use their imaginations

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.451
and to open up their minds
to what's possible out there

00:05:40.451 --> 00:05:44.970
and then putting the time
in and working for it.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.730
So all good stuff to kind of set goals

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:54.730
and encourage the kids to
really go after what they want

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.040
and put some work or effort into it,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.833
and you never know what can happen.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.110
- Absolutely, it's that
combination of the hard work

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:09.213
and the dreams that
clearly have taken you far.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.580
So you've written three books

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.540
and you are also working with
early childhood literacy.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.290
How did you get into
that from figure skating?

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:22.943
You started quite early
in your career as well.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.610
- Yeah, so my organization Always Dream

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.090
was established in 1996.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:35.130
And we are all about
embracing the hopes and dreams

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.720
of underserved children.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.480
And the last 10 years, we've
really focused our efforts

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.460
on the early childhood literacy.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.090
And I think really the
inspiration behind that

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.630
was being a mom, becoming a mom myself.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.600
I had, I still have two daughters.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.670
But at the time they were four and six,

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.803
so kind of at that learn-to-read age,

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:00.330
had an incredible, high interest in books

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.030
and just reading at home.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.400
And I think my husband and I just saw

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.510
the incredible value that that had

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.970
and the pure benefits
that they received from it

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.126
as they were starting
to get ready for school.

00:07:14.126 --> 00:07:18.020
And I think looking around in our country

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.310
and some of the statistics
of 60% of low income families

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.783
don't have age-appropriate
books in the home,

00:07:25.829 --> 00:07:30.829
and close to 40% of our fourth
graders in the United States

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.790
are reading at or above grade level.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.650
Those are numbers we
really want to change.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.670
And I felt like that was something

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.660
I was really passionate about

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:47.173
and wanted to really turn our focus on.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.010
- Absolutely, and now you're
working with school districts

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.910
and using technology to
help get those resources

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:56.743
into classrooms.

00:07:56.743 --> 00:07:59.300
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.249
- Yeah, so we have a program
called Always Reading.

00:08:02.249 --> 00:08:05.350
And it's focused on

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.640
pre-kindergarten and
kindergarten age students.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.880
And we're primarily in Title I schools,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:17.480
so where resources are very much needed,

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:21.860
and provide one-to-one tablet with ebooks.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.860
So wide range of selection
of titles and subjects

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.605
for the kids to pick from.

00:08:29.605 --> 00:08:33.440
But we also pair that with
family engagement support.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.650
And with that support, we
reach out to the families

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.790
three times a week with text messages.

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:43.790
And there's everything from
reading tips and strategies

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:45.650
to little gentle reminders,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.890
like, "Hey, there's 10
new books in your library.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.047
Why don't you pick one out and
read to your child tonight?"

00:08:52.047 --> 00:08:56.180
And sometimes questions
to maybe ask your kids

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.963
as you're reading a
book with them together.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.980
It's a program that we
really, really love to see

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.360
the change in behavior
of the families at home,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.432
that they do set up a
literacy-rich environment

00:09:10.432 --> 00:09:15.017
with their child and spend
that time engaging with them

00:09:15.017 --> 00:09:18.060
and just understand the
importance and the impact

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.890
that that will have on their
child as they move forward

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:23.173
in their education.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:25.313
- That's great.

00:09:25.313 --> 00:09:28.490
And I love that you have
implemented these tips.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:32.260
Do some of them come from
tips that you've acquired

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.853
as a mom as you were
teaching your kids to read?

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.440
- Definitely, I think
we do everything we can

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.550
to get them interested in books, right?

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.620
And sometimes it's not easy.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:48.500
But I think keeping it fun
is always a great thing.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.300
And even if it means just
picture walking a book

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.017
with your child, like looking
at a picture and asking them,

00:09:56.017 --> 00:09:58.630
"Oh hey, there's 'Dream Big, Little Pig!'

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.610
What color is her dress?"

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.810
Or "What is she doing, is
she playing basketball?"

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.810
And then it prompts them
to kinda think and answer

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.550
and really get more involved
with what they're reading.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:15.463
So keeping it fun was one thing for sure.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.100
- Absolutely, kids are always playing

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.370
and learn the best through play.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.133
So I love that playful
approach to learning.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.963
Tell us about the children's
books that you've written.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:31.410
- Yes, no, absolutely.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.710
So there's three books.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:36.720
One is called "Dream Big Little Pig!"

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:37.553
There it is.

00:10:37.553 --> 00:10:41.660
Poppy the Pig, as you can see,
is interested in ice skating.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.300
She tries a few different things

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.600
before she finds ice skating.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.520
And once she finds ice skating,
she discovers she loves it.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.170
And so this one's all about persistence

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:56.480
and not giving up and
going after your dreams

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.540
even though there's
challenges in front of you.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.357
And then I also did a
follow-up from this called

00:11:02.357 --> 00:11:04.470
"It's a Big World, Little Pig!"

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:09.240
And then "Cara's Kindness"
is about a caring cat

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.896
and she passes kindness forward

00:11:11.896 --> 00:11:15.500
and only asks that her
friends do the same thing.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:19.980
And you never know the
kindness can come full circle

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:20.813
back to you.

00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:25.250
So each one has a little
bit of a positive message

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.890
that hopefully kids can take away

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:32.650
and maybe implement in their own lives.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.440
- Yeah, and speaking of facing challenges,

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:39.990
we have a question from
Kushi from YouTube.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:44.270
And she was asking, "How
do you keep going on

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.950
in spite of having low
scores or performance?"

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:50.423
I'm assuming in regards to reading.

00:11:51.777 --> 00:11:54.303
Do you have some ideas for us?

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.893
- Maybe a parent or maybe
a teacher, sounds like?

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.810
You know, I mean, I think
it's like finding something

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:09.360
that will inspire that child
to really pick up books

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.720
as much as possible.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.720
And I think it's important
to have options of choice

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.120
because when a child's
able to pick and choose,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.600
they take a little bit of
ownership of what they're reading.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.115
And I think that makes
it a little more fun.

00:12:25.115 --> 00:12:30.115
It might prompt them to read
that book again and again,

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.970
which is always great.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:34.867
Or even maybe inspire
them to find other books

00:12:34.867 --> 00:12:36.853
in that same subject.

00:12:39.545 --> 00:12:43.780
I think choice and letting
them pick is a big key

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:45.532
to getting them interested.

00:12:45.532 --> 00:12:49.800
And maybe finding other fun ways

00:12:52.351 --> 00:12:53.750
to get them reading.

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:57.300
Maybe even be outside and
if something interests them,

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.837
like they see a truck
go by and they're like,

00:12:58.837 --> 00:13:00.620
"Wow, look at that big truck,"

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:03.222
and you can say, "Let's go
find some books about trucks

00:13:03.222 --> 00:13:05.790
and learn more about them."

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:08.275
So little tricks like that, I think,

00:13:08.275 --> 00:13:12.790
are great ways to get at a
child's interest in books.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.080
Even if they don't realize

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:15.930
they're learning something, they are.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.238
- Absolutely, especially for
the youngest kids, I think,

00:13:20.238 --> 00:13:23.050
the songs and the rhymes

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:27.080
have so much phonemic awareness
skills that you can build

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:31.610
as you're singing those
songs and playing games, too,

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:32.773
with young kids.

00:13:34.097 --> 00:13:36.430
So yeah, love that idea
of having choice, I think,

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:40.534
with books, hopefully
now, kids can find books

00:13:40.534 --> 00:13:43.897
with subject matter interests
that they're interested in

00:13:43.897 --> 00:13:45.113
and that can go on.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.713
We have another question
from Sam Lewis from YouTube.

00:13:53.697 --> 00:13:56.820
"How do you press on when
your faith is being tested

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:58.800
beyond all you can give?"

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.620
And I hear him, from all these
times during the pandemic,

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:05.090
especially with young children at home.

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:06.623
These are challenging times.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.010
Do you have thoughts for Sam?

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:13.220
- Yes, that's a tough one.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:15.833
And I think it's
something that we all face

00:14:15.833 --> 00:14:18.170
at some point in our lives.

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:23.170
And it's maybe finding
ways to dig deep and see

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.800
maybe what we do have instead of thinking

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:31.403
of all of the challenges and
the hard things in front of us,

00:14:32.323 --> 00:14:35.630
taking stock in some
of the positive things

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.750
that we do have and letting
that lift you up a little more.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:42.810
I know it's not necessarily
going to change your situation,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:47.810
but sometimes it's just a
different perspective mentally

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:52.360
can sometimes bring about
a little bit of change

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:56.323
or inspire change in a different way.

00:14:58.654 --> 00:14:59.510
But it is tough.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:02.120
I think leaning on people
that are close to you

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:05.480
and that you have a trust in.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:07.900
And I think don't be afraid to reach out

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:12.900
because there are some incredibly
generous people out there

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.830
in their hearts where
they definitely want to

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:23.263
give whatever advice or tips to help out.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.382
- Thank you.

00:15:27.382 --> 00:15:28.648
(dog barking)

00:15:28.648 --> 00:15:30.290
- We have another question from-

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:32.113
- There goes my little dog.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:34.903
- Do we get to see your dog?

00:15:36.875 --> 00:15:41.163
- Dog mom here, so he
must see someone outside.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.023
- We are all dog fans here on our team.

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:50.610
Another question from
Dia Salmo on YouTube,

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:54.033
what inspired you to be a figure skater?

00:15:54.951 --> 00:15:59.951
- Ah yes, so basically I
had seen an ice skating show

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:05.210
when I was younger, and I loved the music

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:08.490
and the performances and
the costumes, of course,

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:09.760
as a little girl.

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:13.130
And I think there was
so much about skating

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:14.350
that was magical.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.270
And when I begged my mom to take me,

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:20.110
actually something that
ties in so perfect here

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:23.000
is she said, "Okay, well, when you're six

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.710
and you learn to read,

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:27.610
then I will take you to the ice rink."

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:30.180
So that was big motivation for me

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.910
to get reading and everything.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:36.330
And from the first time I
tried it at six years old,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:40.440
I just absolutely loved it
and kept asking to go back.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.740
And I think the freedom on the ice

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:46.550
and the feeling of movement.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:49.350
Kind of like skiing, you
feel the wind in your face

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.960
and your hair and there's just something

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.440
exhilarating about that.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:58.170
And learning to do the jumps
and the different moves

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.190
on the ice was always a challenge,

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:04.670
but obviously incrementally
just great rewards

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:06.400
and that feeling of accomplishment

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.787
when I was able to learn something new.

00:17:08.787 --> 00:17:13.003
So yeah, lots of things
inspired the skating.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:14.900
- That's fascinating,

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:18.030
and I love that it was tied
to reading from the beginning.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.425
Clearly your mom shared the
same values that you do.

00:17:21.425 --> 00:17:23.093
- Yes, for sure.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.833
- What were your favorite
books at that age?

00:17:28.868 --> 00:17:33.210
- Ooh, okay, good question.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.043
I'm trying to think.

00:17:34.043 --> 00:17:38.687
Well, I had this one little
book called, "Bear's Birthday."

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:42.540
And it wasn't by a big publisher.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:45.363
I think it was actually
like one of those books

00:17:45.363 --> 00:17:47.876
given out at Hallmark or something.

00:17:47.876 --> 00:17:50.960
But it had a little bear
that went on an adventure

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.100
on his birthday and you can
pull him out of each page

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:56.770
and stick him into the new environment

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:58.860
on the consecutive pages.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.193
So it was always a lot of fun.

00:18:03.185 --> 00:18:05.235
I'm trying to think of other little books

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.970
as a kid that age.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:12.410
I mean, I remember,
maybe a little bit older,

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:14.600
loving "Charlotte's Web,"

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:18.050
and yes, you see a pig connection there.

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:20.365
I did have a big thing about pigs.

00:18:20.365 --> 00:18:24.158
So of course, "Charlotte's
Web" was one of my favorites

00:18:24.158 --> 00:18:26.463
as I was more middle school age.

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.130
- Yeah, well, you know, we've seen,

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:34.406
and Yu Wang from YouTube asks us,

00:18:34.406 --> 00:18:35.960
"What do you think is the reason

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.600
that little kids like
to read the same book

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:41.080
over and over again?"

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.376
And related, "How can we get them

00:18:42.376 --> 00:18:44.637
to explore different books?"

00:18:45.527 --> 00:18:49.310
- You know, actually it's
a good thing to read books

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:50.630
over and over again.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:55.630
The repetition is so good
for kids in that age range.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:59.590
They'll hear the same words over and over

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:03.600
and starting to comprehend
things a little bit more.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:07.120
So I definitely encourage
reading books over and over.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:10.233
And we actually do that in
our Always Reading program.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.994
And if that's what they wanna read,

00:19:13.994 --> 00:19:16.620
or have read to them,

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:19.113
then at least they're
hearing something, right?

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:23.000
But I think if you find another book

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:28.000
with a similar character
or subject matter,

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:29.667
you can introduce that and say,

00:19:29.667 --> 00:19:33.070
"Hey, this is just like
such-and-such book.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:35.880
Let's see if there's a
new adventure in this

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.717
that you'd be interested in."

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.260
I think no matter what it is,

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:44.236
if you can get them interested in books,

00:19:44.236 --> 00:19:46.860
that's the key.

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.516
But yeah, don't worry
about the repetition.

00:19:49.516 --> 00:19:51.283
That's always a good thing.

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:53.830
- Absolutely, I'm there with you.

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:56.976
I remember the days when
our kids were young,

00:19:56.976 --> 00:20:00.640
and for parents it seems
very, very repetitive.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:02.590
I remember reading the
same books over and over,

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:03.593
night after night.

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:07.670
But in our work, we've
also noticed that children,

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:08.750
they'll read the same book,

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.470
but they'll be looking at the pictures

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.110
and if you ask them questions,

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:14.460
they may say something
different the next time.

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:19.140
And then sometimes we've
seen that children start,

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:22.830
even pre-reading, they will
start reciting the book,

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.470
because they'll remember it.

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:28.217
That's all good and part of learning.

00:20:28.217 --> 00:20:29.050
- Yes, definitely.

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:33.960
- But like the tips on exploring
different books as well.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.537
Sam Lewis from YouTube has a question.

00:20:38.537 --> 00:20:42.420
"May I ask how to keep
a two-year-old still

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.277
long enough to read to?"

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.610
- Oh, (laughs) let's see,
it's been a long time

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:50.740
since my kids were two.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:52.683
They're 16 and 18 now.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.660
You know, let him sit on
your lap, or her, him or her,

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:02.123
and look at the book, feel the book.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:04.850
Let them take charge.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.640
I think sometimes let them turn the page.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:10.960
Maybe say, "Hey, can you,"

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:12.410
for example, "Goodnight Moon,"

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:16.290
'cause that was one that
our kids wanted to hear

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:17.520
over and over and over,

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.190
and of course find the
little mouse on every page.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:23.287
So maybe make it a little
bit of a game there, like,

00:21:23.287 --> 00:21:25.350
"Oh, can you find the mouse on this page?"

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:27.110
And it kind of occupies them.

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:29.960
I know two years old is maybe a little,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.379
eh, I think they would understand that.

00:21:32.379 --> 00:21:34.963
But yeah, I think it's,

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:41.140
even five minutes is
enough to kinda start that.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:43.070
And as they get older, they might,

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:45.630
you know, you could probably
build up to 10 minutes a day.

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:49.980
And even 10 minutes a day is
gonna make a huge difference

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:53.530
in creating a really strong foundation

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:56.583
for literacy habits and skills.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:01.060
- And sometimes we can
borrow from teachers,

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:04.110
who are so good about setting
up those reading routines

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.600
in the school day.

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:06.433
Sometimes at home,

00:22:06.433 --> 00:22:09.400
parents can set aside
some time for reading.

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:12.550
So after dinner or before bed,

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:15.400
children are growing
up expecting that time.

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:17.540
That sometimes helps, too,

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:21.314
when children get the routine.

00:22:21.314 --> 00:22:22.993
- Yes, yes, they do.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.127
- Sharon from Facebook asked us,

00:22:27.127 --> 00:22:30.620
"Do you have a variety
of genres you prefer

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:31.970
for your personal reading?"

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:37.520
- Ooh, yeah, I kind of
bounce all over the map.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:41.723
I mean, lately I've kind
of been going back to some,

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:46.130
more action and thriller.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:48.100
I like a lot of spy novels

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:50.750
and kind of the

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:55.270
CIA-type suspense ones.

00:22:55.270 --> 00:23:00.270
But then I read a couple of
great ones over the summer.

00:23:03.297 --> 00:23:04.590
"Where the Crawdads Sing,"

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:06.550
I'm not sure if you guys
are familiar with that.

00:23:06.550 --> 00:23:08.413
It was an incredible book.

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:14.510
So yeah, I kind of like to
explore all different things.

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:19.510
But I know I definitely love
adventure, and even fantasy.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:21.560
Like "Lord of the
Rings," that whole series

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:25.083
was one of my favorite growing up.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:31.700
- Great, so maybe now
going back to your past

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:36.700
as a kid of children in our demographic,

00:23:38.145 --> 00:23:42.330
what was your training like
when you were first starting out

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:43.833
at six to eight years old?

00:23:44.993 --> 00:23:48.220
- So you know, I mean, at six to eight,

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:51.200
I was still kind of getting
to know skating a little bit.

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:56.200
So it was maybe going a couple
times a week after school,

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:59.380
or whatever, and them maybe building up

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:01.860
to a few times a week.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:06.860
So it wasn't real strenuous
training at that point.

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:11.810
Maybe a little bit older,

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:13.970
when I was closer to middle school age,

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:17.150
then I started going a
little bit before school.

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:19.530
So that's when the early mornings started

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:23.810
where my parents, or my
mom would get up early,

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:26.020
take me for an hour or two before school,

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:27.490
and then sometimes after school.

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:31.360
So I think that's more 11,

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.920
12 is when I started to get more serious

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:38.920
and committed to really
focusing in on more training.

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:43.410
- And then over the years as you competed,

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:47.258
how did you build up a mental
toughness that you needed

00:24:47.258 --> 00:24:52.258
to prepare for the pressure
of being at the Olympics

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:54.603
and being on a worldwide stage?

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:58.610
- I mean, it comes gradually, right?

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:03.610
But I think my coach was really
incredible, Christy Ness,

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:07.160
at really preparing her students

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.380
to mentally be tough in competition.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:13.810
And she was a great technician and trainer

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:15.360
that had us physically prepared,

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:19.210
but also wanted us to be
mentally tough as well.

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:22.090
So she was tough on us in training.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:24.483
Like she never let up.

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:30.003
I wouldn't say too much
tough love, but a little bit.

00:25:32.091 --> 00:25:34.700
She would try to get us into that feeling

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:39.700
and that atmosphere of a little
bit of nerves in practice

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.600
once in a while, especially
when we would perform,

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:46.170
practice our routines,

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:49.770
just so that when we felt
those nerves in competition,

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:53.011
it wasn't something that was new

00:25:53.011 --> 00:25:56.540
and uncomfortable.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.370
Well, it was uncomfortable,

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:00.260
but you knew how to deal with it

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:02.940
and you knew that, okay, even
though I feel a little shaky,

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:07.313
I can still go out there and perform okay.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:14.080
- And what was it like
being one of the first

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:17.453
Asian American figure
skaters representing the US?

00:26:19.932 --> 00:26:22.880
- I think I feel more of the impact now

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:24.280
than I did at the time.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.413
You know, at the time, I didn't know that.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.020
I knew that at the very,
very top world level,

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.410
there were very few Asian Americans.

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.501
At the time, Tiffany Chin,

00:26:37.501 --> 00:26:41.090
who won a bronze world medal

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:43.610
and was US National
Champion in the early '80s

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:48.250
was one of my big idols and role models.

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:52.530
So I definitely looked at her
and I felt that connection

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.380
because she was also Asian American

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:57.563
and a California girl like me.

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.690
So I didn't really feel like,

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:03.690
oh, I'm trying to blaze a trail here,

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:05.541
like, oh, there's a lot of pressure.

00:27:05.541 --> 00:27:09.050
I probably didn't really
realize what had happened

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:12.120
until after the Olympics

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:15.190
and when it was brought
more to my attention.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:17.340
Like, oh hey, you're
the first Asian American

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:21.272
to win a gold in the Winter Olympics.

00:27:21.272 --> 00:27:22.220
I was like, oh, really?

00:27:22.220 --> 00:27:23.223
Okay, that's cool.

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:26.740
And I think when I saw

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:31.200
how much it meant to the
Asian American community,

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:34.003
I think that's when I
realized like, oh wow.

00:27:36.819 --> 00:27:38.510
This does mean something.

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:41.820
And I think it made me reflect a lot more

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:46.820
and be even more appreciative
to the history of my family

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:51.860
here in the US and really the sacrifices

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.580
and the trail that they blazed

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:56.363
so that I could really
live that American dream.

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:01.420
- Wonderful, well, it's just
been such an inspiration

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:03.320
to have this conversation with you

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:06.080
and to hear about all your,

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:09.280
not only your Olympic history,

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:13.780
but also your deep work with
children in early literacy.

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:17.500
So thank you so much,
Kristi, for taking the time

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.867
and sharing with our audience.

00:28:19.867 --> 00:28:20.950
- Absolutely.

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:24.320
Thank you, Caroline, it
was so great to talk to you

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:25.730
and an honor to talk to you, too,

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:29.250
and just a huge fan of Khan Academy Kids

00:28:29.250 --> 00:28:33.340
and just love what you
guys do, too, to encourage

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:36.403
all of that early education
because it's so important.

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:38.710
- Well, thank you so much.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:42.802
And if folks want to see this,

00:28:42.802 --> 00:28:47.802
just find this podcast
wherever you get your podcasts

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.450
and look for Khan Academy Ed Talks

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:54.380
and we will see you next time.

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:55.213
Thank you!

